Yes. Without planning it as one product, we've built a single repeatable video maker. Every project uses the same five steps. What changes between projects is which engine fills each step.

The pipeline every project shares

1. SPEC / SCRIPT   ->  2. ENGINE (Python)   ->  3. MOTION (Remotion/React)  ->  4. RENDER     ->  5. FINISH (ffmpeg)
   what to make         makes the data,          draws the data as frames       batch it         grade, loudness,
   (JSON or text)       art, voice, sound                                                        encode -> out/
That's why every project ends up with the same folders.

Folder	Holds
specs/, episodes/ or stories/	Step 1: one JSON or text file per video, with all the on-screen text
engine/	Step 2: physics, art, voice and sound
motion/	Step 3: the Remotion project
batch.py, run_*.ps1 or render_all.ps1	Step 4
finish.py or polish.py	Step 5
out/	Finished videos
posts.csv	Captions and hashtags ready to post
How each project fills the five steps:

Project	Step 2: engine	Step 3: what draws the frames	Step 5: finish
Instavideos (and viral-shorts, weapon-wars)	Physics sims tried over many random seeds; the most dramatic run is kept. All sound and music are synthesised in code.	Remotion draws the sim data	ffmpeg grade, −14 LUFS, encoded on the GPU (NVENC)
Panchtantra	FLUX paints the art on your GPU; CLIP picks the best image; edge-tts narrates	A custom GPU compositor (PyTorch on CUDA), not Remotion	NVENC
Known Issues	Same FLUX art pipeline, plus edge-tts voices and a synth score	A custom Python/PIL compositor on the CPU	CPU encode (x264)
Promo films (RecapHub, Flayel)	Real product captures, beat-timed scripts, synth music	Remotion films built on a beat grid	EffectCraft polish or ffmpeg
Frame-rendered series (physics-vault, chemistry-lab, blender-fusion)	shortkit/kit.py shared helpers; Blender for the 3D	Python writes frames straight to ffmpeg	daily-posts/seamless.py makes each short loop with no restart pause
