Most of Remotion. Remotion takes a screenshot in Chrome for every frame, and the CPU is the bottleneck there; the GPU sits around 10%.
Earlier: the finish step was x264 on the CPU. Moving it to NVENC was the "shift to GPU".
Not local at all: the voices (edge-tts) come from Microsoft's online neural voice service.
On "200 videos in 5 minutes": that isn't what happens. The measured figure in instavideos/CLAUDE.md is about 33 s to render plus 14 s to finish per short. The two steps overlap, so 161 shorts take about 1.5 hours. That's still fast, about 35 seconds per short unattended, but nowhere near 5 minutes for 200. Running more than 8 render tabs didn't help, because the CPU is the limit.

The gap with the Teamble video, honestly
You're right that cutting it frame by frame won't close it; I tried that twice. Our films are good data-driven video. Teamble is hand-animated motion design, and that's a different kind of work. Here's what it has that ours don't:

Every element moves on its own. In Teamble, inside one shot the card arrives, then its avatar pops, the text types, the counter ticks and the button pulses, all staggered over a few frames. Ours usually move the whole screenshot with one zoom or crop. That's the biggest difference you feel.
Easing tuned per move. A motion designer adjusts the curve of each move: a little overshoot, a soft landing, a held moment. We reuse the same spring everywhere, which reads as "template".
Real motion blur. Every camera move and the cursor get blurred in proportion to speed. We have never turned it on. Remotion has @remotion/motion-blur, but it makes renders 5–10× slower.
A designed camera and transitions. One continuous camera with depth between layers, and every cut is a designed match cut (an element becomes the next shot). Ours are mostly hard cuts and fades.
Produced music, edited to the picture, plus dense sound effects. Their music comes from a professional library and is cut to the shots; there's a whoosh, click or pop on almost every event. Ours is synthesised in numpy with few effects. Sound is roughly half of the "expensive" feel.
3D assets. The glossy cursor and orb are real 3D renders. We have Blender for this but haven't made them yet.
The review loop. This is the real one. An editor watches a shot at full speed, nudges it by 2 frames, and repeats hundreds of times. I review mostly from still contact sheets, then render once. Stills can't show timing or easing, and those are exactly where the polish lives.
So it's not missing software. It's design (simplified UI pieces; now done in the style frames, matched to your brand), motion craft (points 1–4), sound (point 5) and the iteration loop (point 7).

How I'd close it
Animatic: the approved style frames placed on the music's beats, to lock timing before any polish.
One shot at a time: build each shot with every element animated separately. Render a 3–5 s clip and put it side by side with the matching Teamble clip, then tune timing and easing until the two feel the same, before moving on. This replaces my still-only checks.
Turn on motion blur for the final render only.
Sound: a licensed track you approve, plus a real SFX library placed on every event.
Blender: render the 3D cursor and logo on the 5070.
Points 1–3 and 5 I can do. Point 4 needs you to pick and license the music. The judgement "does it feel right" needs your eyes on the side-by-side clips, which is where a real editor's taste comes in.

